    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1078">

                <pb facs="1435" n="1425" sameAs="frus:frus1945v07/pg1425"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.6363/1–1345: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the Ambassador in
                        China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Hurley</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-01-13T20:00:00-04:00">January 13, 1945—8
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>74. Following the request of the National Resources Commission of China we have
                    arranged with the Petroleum Administration for War to send to China two
                    petroleum experts, Martin J. Gavin and Andrew N. Mackenzie, for the purpose of
                    obtaining full data as to what immediate steps might be taken to maintain and
                    expand the production, refining, and distribution facilities of the Kansu Oil
                    Fields so as to achieve the maximum potential supply of oil from this source for
                    war needs in that theatre.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d1078-f1"/> They will leave here
                    about February 1, 1945. Please inform the Chinese Government and the U. S.
                    Military Commander and advise the Department if these arrangements are
                    agreeable. For your guidance the military authorities here have expressed their
                    definite interest in securing this information.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1078-f1" n="2" xml:id="d1078-f1-content">
                <p>The interest of the Chinese Government in the mission was expressed in a letter
                    of December 4, 1944, from Dr. C. S. Wang, Director of the Technical Office in
                    the United States of the National Resources Commission of China
                    (893.6363/1–1245).</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
